<p class="noind">At \(\rho = 0.20\), an unloaded joint dissipates 10.9 times the heat of an uncorrupted
command while altering joint motion by only 1.8%. In contrast, a joint supporting a 20 N&middot;m
static load exhibits merely a 2% thermal increase under the same command roughness. Under unloaded
conditions, dissipation follows</p>

<div class="eqn">
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{H} \;=\; \mathcal{H}_0 \;+\; \kappa\,\frac{\rho}{1-\rho}
\end{equation}
</div>

<p class="noind">with \(\kappa = 38.4\) and a maximum residual of 0.45%. The crossover where posture
loss equals ripple loss, \(\bar{\tau}^2 = \mathrm{Var}(\tau)\), emerges at 3.2 N&middot;m.</p>

<p>On the Unitree Go1, measured mean absolute joint torques \(|\bar{\tau}|\) average
1.24&ndash;1.48 N&middot;m for hip abduction, 2.00&ndash;2.59 N&middot;m for thighs, and
5.88&ndash;6.88 N&middot;m for calves. Eight of the twelve joints operate below the 3.2 N&middot;m
crossover, while the four knee actuators operate well above it. Consequently, posture demands dominate
knee dissipation, whereas jitter drives overheating in lightly loaded joints.</p>

